[
{"id":"3ff5e44e-9557-402e-bed3-76a23ce2a8c6","ref":"2.2 Ex 2.2 Q.1 (i)","solution":"Let \\(A = \\begin{bmatrix} -1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 \\end{bmatrix}\\). For a \\(2\\times 2\\) matrix the co-factor \\(A_{ij} = (-1)^{i+j} M_{ij}\\), where \\(M_{ij}\\) is the minor.\n\n\\(A_{11} = +4 = 4\\)\n\\(A_{12} = -(-3) = 3\\)\n\\(A_{21} = -(2) = -2\\)\n\\(A_{22} = +(-1) = -1\\)\n\nSo the co-factors are \\(A_{11}=4,\\ A_{12}=3,\\ A_{21}=-2,\\ A_{22}=-1\\)."},
{"id":"d3f17a62-fcfa-4446-a92b-38c5602ea55a","ref":"2.2 Ex 2.2 Q.1 (ii)","solution":"Let \\(A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}\\). Each co-factor is \\(A_{ij} = (-1)^{i+j} M_{ij}\\).\n\nRow 1:\n\\(A_{11} = +\\begin{vmatrix} 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\end{vmatrix} = -3\\)\n\\(A_{12} = -\\begin{vmatrix} -2 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1 \\end{vmatrix} = -(2+10) = -12\\)\n\\(A_{13} = +\\begin{vmatrix} -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\end{vmatrix} = (0+6) = 6\\)\n\nRow 2:\n\\(A_{21} = -\\begin{vmatrix} -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\end{vmatrix} = -(1) = -1\\)\n\\(A_{22} = +\\begin{vmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 \\end{vmatrix} = (-1+4) = 3\\)\n\\(A_{23} = -\\begin{vmatrix} 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\end{vmatrix} = -(0-2) = 2\\)\n\nRow 3:\n\\(A_{31} = +\\begin{vmatrix} -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 5 \\end{vmatrix} = (-5-6) = -11\\)\n\\(A_{32} = -\\begin{vmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 5 \\end{vmatrix} = -(5+4) = -9\\)\n\\(A_{33} = +\\begin{vmatrix} 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 \\end{vmatrix} = (3-2) = 1\\)\n\nCo-factors: \\(A_{11}=-3,\\ A_{12}=-12,\\ A_{13}=6,\\ A_{21}=-1,\\ A_{22}=3,\\ A_{23}=2,\\ A_{31}=-11,\\ A_{32}=-9,\\ A_{33}=1\\)."},
{"id":"b2f59942-41b7-4479-a716-a34446cdb18e","ref":"2.2 Ex 2.2 Q.2 (i)","solution":"Let \\(A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}\\). Compute the co-factors \\(A_{ij}=(-1)^{i+j}M_{ij}\\):\n\n\\(A_{11} = +(-1) = -1\\)\n\\(A_{12} = -(4) = -4\\)\n\\(A_{21} = -(3) = -3\\)\n\\(A_{22} = +(1) = 1\\)\n\nMatrix of co-factors: \\(\\begin{bmatrix} -1 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}\\)."},
{"id":"82bd56ed-71b3-4e54-bfe6-7c972d8b8f79","ref":"2.2 Ex 2.2 Q.2 (ii)","solution":"Let \\(A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -5 \\end{bmatrix}\\). Compute each co-factor \\(A_{ij}=(-1)^{i+j}M_{ij}\\):\n\nRow 1: \\(A_{11}=+(1\\cdot(-5)-3\\cdot 3)=-14\\), \\(A_{12}=-((-2)(-5)-3\\cdot 0)=-10\\), \\(A_{13}=+((-2)(3)-1\\cdot 0)=-6\\)\nRow 2: \\(A_{21}=-((0)(-5)-2\\cdot 3)=6\\), \\(A_{22}=+((1)(-5)-2\\cdot 0)=-5\\), \\(A_{23}=-((1)(3)-0\\cdot 0)=-3\\)\nRow 3: \\(A_{31}=+((0)(3)-2\\cdot 1)=-2\\), \\(A_{32}=-((1)(3)-2\\cdot(-2))=-7\\), \\(A_{33}=+((1)(1)-0\\cdot(-2))=1\\)\n\nMatrix of co-factors: \\(\\begin{bmatrix} -14 & -10 & -6 \\\\ 6 & -5 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -7 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}\\)."},
{"id":"760aa0f5-e4b5-49e5-800f-ebf3aacfc44d","ref":"2.2 Ex 2.2 Q.3 (i)","solution":"Let \\(A = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 5 \\end{bmatrix}\\). For a \\(2\\times 2\\) matrix \\(\\begin{bmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{bmatrix}\\), the adjoint is \\(\\begin{bmatrix} d & -b \\\\ -c & a \\end{bmatrix}\\) (interchange the diagonal entries, change the sign of the off-diagonal entries).\n\n\\(\\text{adj}(A) = \\begin{bmatrix} 5 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}\\)."},
{"id":"a16c016a-ecd8-4a30-a9e0-cf12c3b99835","ref":"2.2 Ex 2.2 Q.3 (ii)","solution":"Let \\(A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}\\). The adjoint is the transpose of the co-factor matrix, \\(\\text{adj}(A) = [A_{ij}]^{T}\\).\n\nCo-factors:\n\\(A_{11}=-3,\\ A_{12}=-12,\\ A_{13}=6\\)\n\\(A_{21}=-1,\\ A_{22}=3,\\ A_{23}=2\\)\n\\(A_{31}=-11,\\ A_{32}=-9,\\ A_{33}=1\\)\n\nCo-factor matrix \\([A_{ij}] = \\begin{bmatrix} -3 & -12 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -11 & -9 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}\\).\n\nTaking the transpose:\n\\(\\text{adj}(A) = \\begin{bmatrix} -3 & -1 & -11 \\\\ -12 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 6 & 2 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}\\)."},
{"id":"42902135-54fd-47aa-88c2-9070b03ba60f","ref":"2.2 Ex 2.2 Q.4","solution":"Let \\(A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}\\).\n\nDeterminant (expanding along column 2, which has entries \\(-1,0,0\\)):\n\\(|A| = -(-1)\\begin{vmatrix} 3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\end{vmatrix} = 1\\cdot(9+2) = 11\\).\n\nCo-factors:\n\\(A_{11}=+(0)=0,\\ A_{12}=-(9+2)=-11,\\ A_{13}=+(0)=0\\)\n\\(A_{21}=-(-3-0)=3,\\ A_{22}=+(3-2)=1,\\ A_{23}=-(0+1)=-1\\)\n\\(A_{31}=+(2-0)=2,\\ A_{32}=-(-2-6)=8,\\ A_{33}=+(0+3)=3\\)\n\nCo-factor matrix \\(\\begin{bmatrix} 0 & -11 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}\\), so \\(\\text{adj}(A) = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -11 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}\\).\n\nNow verify:\n\\(A\\,(\\text{adj}\\,A) = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -11 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 11 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 11 \\end{bmatrix}\\).\n\n\\((\\text{adj}\\,A)\\,A = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -11 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 11 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 11 \\end{bmatrix}\\).\n\nAnd \\(|A|\\,I = 11\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 11 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 11 \\end{bmatrix}\\).\n\nHence \\(A(\\text{adj}\\,A) = (\\text{adj}\\,A)A = |A|\\,I\\), as required."},
{"id":"35dfa842-0bc2-4e73-82fb-4000d3fd8dfa","ref":"2.2 Ex 2.2 Q.5 (i)","solution":"Let \\(A = \\begin{bmatrix} -1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}\\). By the adjoint method \\(A^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{|A|}\\,\\text{adj}(A)\\).\n\n\\(|A| = (-1)(2) - (5)(-3) = -2 + 15 = 13 \\neq 0\\), so \\(A^{-1}\\) exists.\n\n\\(\\text{adj}(A) = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}\\).\n\n\\(A^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}\\)."},
{"id":"45de40df-2d3b-4560-baac-4aa5d6ca7748","ref":"2.2 Ex 2.2 Q.5 (ii)","solution":"Let \\(A = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}\\). By the adjoint method \\(A^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{|A|}\\,\\text{adj}(A)\\).\n\n\\(|A| = (2)(3) - (-2)(4) = 6 + 8 = 14 \\neq 0\\).\n\n\\(\\text{adj}(A) = \\begin{bmatrix} 3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}\\).\n\n\\(A^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix} 3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}\\)."},
{"id":"bfbf3329-3c43-4caa-9973-d6c2e4ffd1fa","ref":"2.2 Ex 2.2 Q.5 (iii)","solution":"Let \\(A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}\\). This is lower triangular, so \\(|A| = (1)(3)(-1) = -3 \\neq 0\\).\n\nCo-factors:\n\\(A_{11}=+(3\\cdot(-1)-0)= -3,\\ A_{12}=-(3\\cdot(-1)-0)=3,\\ A_{13}=+(6-15)=-9\\)\n\\(A_{21}=-(0)=0,\\ A_{22}=+(-1-0)=-1,\\ A_{23}=-(2-0)=-2\\)\n\\(A_{31}=+(0)=0,\\ A_{32}=-(0)=0,\\ A_{33}=+(3-0)=3\\)\n\nCo-factor matrix \\(\\begin{bmatrix} -3 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}\\), so \\(\\text{adj}(A) = \\begin{bmatrix} -3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & -2 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}\\).\n\n\\(A^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{-3}\\begin{bmatrix} -3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & -2 & 3 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 3 & \\frac{2}{3} & -1 \\end{bmatrix}\\)."},
{"id":"66092ad0-ad4d-409e-8099-232e83f90a08","ref":"2.2 Ex 2.2 Q.5 (iv)","solution":"Let \\(A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 \\end{bmatrix}\\). This is upper triangular, so \\(|A| = (1)(2)(5) = 10 \\neq 0\\).\n\nCo-factors:\n\\(A_{11}=+(10-0)=10,\\ A_{12}=-(0-0)=0,\\ A_{13}=+(0-0)=0\\)\n\\(A_{21}=-(10-0)=-10,\\ A_{22}=+(5-0)=5,\\ A_{23}=-(0-0)=0\\)\n\\(A_{31}=+(8-6)=2,\\ A_{32}=-(4-0)=-4,\\ A_{33}=+(2-0)=2\\)\n\nCo-factor matrix \\(\\begin{bmatrix} 10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}\\), so \\(\\text{adj}(A) = \\begin{bmatrix} 10 & -10 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}\\).\n\n\\(A^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix} 10 & -10 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}\\)."},
{"id":"9b7662dd-16ba-44a2-8d0f-367e59d4472c","ref":"2.2 Ex 2.2 Q.6 (i)","solution":"Let \\(A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}\\). Using \\(A^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{|A|}\\,\\text{adj}(A)\\).\n\n\\(|A| = (1)(-1) - (2)(2) = -1 - 4 = -5 \\neq 0\\).\n\n\\(\\text{adj}(A) = \\begin{bmatrix} -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}\\).\n\n\\(A^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{-5}\\begin{bmatrix} -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\end{bmatrix} = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}\\)."},
{"id":"654a0ba9-abe9-4061-b606-0ebf1fd5a034","ref":"2.2 Ex 2.2 Q.6 (ii)","solution":"Let \\(A = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}\\). Using \\(A^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{|A|}\\,\\text{adj}(A)\\).\n\n\\(|A| = (2)(2) - (-3)(-1) = 4 - 3 = 1 \\neq 0\\).\n\n\\(\\text{adj}(A) = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}\\).\n\n\\(A^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}\\)."},
{"id":"6a5274d3-873d-4c2e-bc86-dd32b9d8104e","ref":"2.2 Ex 2.2 Q.6 (iii)","solution":"Let \\(A = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}\\), and use \\(A^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{|A|}\\,\\text{adj}(A)\\).\n\nDeterminant (expand along row 1):\n\\(|A| = 0 - 1\\begin{vmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\end{vmatrix} + 2\\begin{vmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\end{vmatrix} = -1(1-9) + 2(1-6) = 8 - 10 = -2 \\neq 0\\).\n\nCo-factors:\n\\(A_{11}=+(2-3)=-1,\\ A_{12}=-(1-9)=8,\\ A_{13}=+(1-6)=-5\\)\n\\(A_{21}=-(1-2)=1,\\ A_{22}=+(0-6)=-6,\\ A_{23}=-(0-3)=3\\)\n\\(A_{31}=+(3-4)=-1,\\ A_{32}=-(0-2)=2,\\ A_{33}=+(0-1)=-1\\)\n\nCo-factor matrix \\(\\begin{bmatrix} -1 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}\\), so \\(\\text{adj}(A) = \\begin{bmatrix} -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 8 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}\\).\n\n\\(A^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{-2}\\begin{bmatrix} -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 8 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3 & -1 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -4 & 3 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\end{bmatrix}\\)."},
{"id":"febb32a5-5ba6-41c8-b49c-3bbfaea8b2bd","ref":"2.2 Ex 2.2 Q.6 (iv)","solution":"Let \\(A = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}\\), and use \\(A^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{|A|}\\,\\text{adj}(A)\\).\n\nDeterminant (expand along row 1):\n\\(|A| = 2\\begin{vmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\end{vmatrix} - 0 + (-1)\\begin{vmatrix} 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{vmatrix} = 2(3) - 1(5) = 6 - 5 = 1 \\neq 0\\).\n\nCo-factors:\n\\(A_{11}=+(3-0)=3,\\ A_{12}=-(15-0)=-15,\\ A_{13}=+(5-0)=5\\)\n\\(A_{21}=-(0+1)=-1,\\ A_{22}=+(6-0)=6,\\ A_{23}=-(2-0)=-2\\)\n\\(A_{31}=+(0+1)=1,\\ A_{32}=-(0+5)=-5,\\ A_{33}=+(2-0)=2\\)\n\nCo-factor matrix \\(\\begin{bmatrix} 3 & -15 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}\\), so \\(\\text{adj}(A) = \\begin{bmatrix} 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -15 & 6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}\\).\n\n\\(A^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix} 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -15 & 6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 2 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -15 & 6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}\\)."}
]
